prompt.en:
In this fictional situation, you organise a language-practice group of six adults. You want a quiet room at the public library for a regular weekend meeting. Write to the library manager. In your letter:
- explain the group and the purpose of its meetings;
- describe the room and access arrangements you need;
- ask about availability, booking rules and any charges.
Use a formal, polite tone. Ask for missing details; do not invent fees or exact dates. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Sir or Madam,

In this fictional situation, you left a grey canvas bag on a train earlier this week. It contained a green notebook and a reusable water bottle. Write to the railway lost-property office. In your letter:
- explain what happened and identify the bag;
- explain why you would like it returned;
- ask how to check whether it has been found and arrange collection.
The notebook contains your own course notes. Do not invent a train number, precise date, monetary value or recovery result. Use a formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Sir or Madam,

In this fictional situation, a community centre has advertised for volunteers at a repair day. You want a non-technical role welcoming visitors. You already help greet people and organise materials at a weekly reading group. Write to the organiser. In your letter:
- explain why the role interests you;
- describe your relevant experience;
- ask about duties and preparation.
You can attend either the morning or afternoon session. Do not invent qualifications, employment history, pay or exact dates. Use a formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Sir or Madam,

In this fictional situation, you use the bus stop beside the community sports centre after a weekly group activity. The stop has neither a shelter nor a bench. Write to the local transport authority. In your letter:
- explain when and why you use the stop;
- describe the difficulties of waiting there in wet weather;
- suggest practical improvements and ask for a response.
Use a formal, constructive tone. You may suggest a shelter and seating, but do not invent accident reports, passenger counts, costs or exact dates. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Sir or Madam,

In this fictional situation, you know your neighbour Alex but are not close friends. You plan to fit shelves on a shared wall on Saturday morning, and some drilling will be needed. Write to Alex. In your letter:
- explain the work and why you are writing in advance;
- acknowledge the possible disturbance and suggest ways to limit it;
- ask whether the proposed morning causes a problem and offer to discuss another time.
Do not invent an agreed time, professional booking, cost or building permission. Use a considerate, semi-formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Alex,

In this fictional situation, your manager is Ms Patel. You give short updates at weekly team meetings and want to attend a presentation workshop on a Wednesday afternoon. Write to her. In your letter:
- explain the skill you want to improve;
- describe how the workshop could help your work;
- request permission to attend and suggest how to organise your tasks that afternoon.
No fee, date or colleague cover has been confirmed. Ask about arrangements instead of inventing them. Use a professional, semi-formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Ms Patel,

In this fictional situation, you rent a flat from Ms Lee, whom you have met several times. The kitchen tap continues to drip when turned off, although it still supplies water normally. Write to her. In your letter:
- describe the problem clearly;
- explain the inconvenience and your concern about wasted water;
- ask for a repair and offer to discuss access to the flat.
You are usually home in the evenings. Do not invent damage, repair bills, a diagnosis or a confirmed visit. Use a polite, semi-formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Ms Lee,

In this fictional situation, you completed Mr Brown's public-speaking course. You prepared group discussion notes and regularly helped classmates practise. You are applying to help lead a community reading group, which asks for a reference. Write to Mr Brown. In your letter:
- remind him of your participation in the course;
- explain the volunteer role and why you are applying;
- ask whether he can provide a reference and what information he needs.
Do not invent grades, awards, a submission deadline or his agreement. Use a respectful, semi-formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Mr Brown,

In this fictional situation, your reading group normally meets in a library room on Tuesday evenings. The room will be unavailable for the next meeting because of repairs. You know the coordinator, Sam. Write to Sam. In your letter:
- explain why the usual meeting cannot go ahead there;
- suggest Thursday evening as an alternative, subject to room availability;
- offer to help check arrangements and inform members once confirmed.
Do not invent an exact date, a new booking or members' consent. Use a cooperative, semi-formal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Sam,

In this fictional situation, you want your friend Jamie to visit your town for two days in early summer. You can offer a spare room, and you would like to visit the local museum and riverside park together. Write to Jamie. In your letter:
- invite your friend and explain why you would enjoy the visit;
- describe accommodation and possible activities;
- ask about suitable dates and anything your friend would like to do.
No dates or tickets are booked. Do not invent prices, addresses or confirmed opening hours. Use an informal, friendly tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Jamie,

In this fictional situation, your friend Chris lent you a bicycle for an agreed shopping trip. A tyre went flat during the trip, and you stopped riding immediately. Write to Chris. In your letter:
- explain what happened;
- apologise and say how you plan to put things right;
- ask whether your repair and return plan would be convenient.
You intend to arrange a repair before returning it, but no booking, cause or cost is confirmed. Do not invent an accident or further damage. Use an informal, considerate tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Chris,

In this fictional situation, you recently joined a beginner cooking group at a community centre. Members practise simple vegetable dishes and share ideas. Your friend Taylor has said they want to learn to cook. Write to Taylor. In your letter:
- describe the group and what you do there;
- explain what you enjoy about learning this way;
- invite your friend to try a session and ask about availability.
The next session time, fees and spare places have not been checked. Do not invent these or claim health results. Use an informal, encouraging tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Taylor,

In this fictional situation, you will be away for a week and hope your friend Morgan can check your flat. Two indoor plants need watering once during that week, and letters should be brought in from the mailbox. Write to Morgan. In your letter:
- explain your request and why you are asking this friend;
- describe the two tasks clearly;
- ask whether they can help and offer to arrange a key handover if they agree.
No exact travel dates or handover are agreed. Do not invent an address, access code, emergency or extra chores. Use an informal, appreciative tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Morgan,

In this fictional situation, your friend Casey helped you move by packing books and labelling boxes. You have now unpacked and settled into your new home. Write to Casey. In your letter:
- thank your friend for the specific help;
- explain how it made the move easier and describe settling in;
- invite your friend for a simple meal and ask when they are free.
Do not invent a paid service, gift value, address, exact date or confirmed visit. Use a warm, informal tone. Write at least 150 words. Begin: Dear Casey,
